          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What does the diagnosis mean, what does guidance describe as first-line therapy, and which code covers it?",
          "answer": "I'm sorry you've received this diagnosis. Here is what the retrieved material says. It is general information, not advice about your own care.\n\n**What the diagnosis means.** The retrieved material does not explain type 2 diabetes in general terms. It covers the treatment target. Diabetes association guidance describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults, mainly to reduce small-vessel complications affecting the eyes, kidneys and nerves. The target is individualized, so a clinician may set a different one for you (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). Your clinician is the right person to explain what the diagnosis means for you.\n\n**First-line therapy.** Current diabetes guidance no longer uses a single fixed sequence. The first choice depends on other conditions you may have, and for several of them it is made without reference to the HbA1c value. Where no other major condition is present, metformin alongside structured lifestyle changes is described as the usual starting agent. The guidance notes that kidney function is checked before starting and periodically afterward (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). This is educational information only. Whether any medicine suits you is a decision for you and your clinician.\n\n**Code.** Type 2 diabetes mellitus is code E11 in ICD-10-CM, in Chapter 4 (endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases). Taking insulin does not change the code; it stays E11 (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11, coding-icd10-chapter-04-endocrine).\n\n**What the results did not cover.** The retrieved material does not describe symptoms, how the condition is diagnosed, lifestyle guidance in detail, or what to expect over time. It also does not say how the first-line choice changes when other conditions are present. Please ask your clinician about these, and about your own target and monitoring schedule.\n\nIf you develop very high blood sugar with confusion, vomiting, trouble breathing, or extreme drowsiness, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
